1. Bit Tricks

static inline int count_bits(U64 bitboard);

The function count_bits works using a trick with binary numbers.  When you subtract 1 from a binary number, it either clears bit 0 or clears the least significant set bit to set all downstream bits to 1.  When a bitwise AND is applied to the original number and that number minus 1, only the bits more significant than the least significant set bit remain the same and are preserved, while the least signficant set bit is cleared.  If we repeat this process until the number is 0 and count the number of times we use the bitwise AND, then we can count the number of bits without having to check every single bit. 

static inline int get_lsb_index(U64 bitboard);

The function get_lsb_index finds the bit index of the least significant set bit.  The input must be greater than zero.  The function works by applying bitwise AND to the number provided and its negation.  The negation of a number is just the complement of that number plus one.  By definition, the least significant set bit is the first 1 bit.  This means that the bits before the first 1 bit are 0 bits.  This means that when the complement is taken, all these 0 bits flip to 1 and the first 1 bit flips to 0.  Adding 1 means that the 1 carries over all the way to the first 1 bit.  This means that while every other bit is flipped, the bits up to the least significant set bit are not.  So when we take a bitwise AND, we preserve only the least significant set bit.  Then, if we subtract 1 from this, we clear the least significant set bit and set all the bits before it to 1.  If we count how many 1 bits there are then we find the index of the least significant set bit. 

2. Move Generation

2.1 Leaper Pieces
These are namely the pawn, knight, and king.

U64 mask_pawn_attacks(int square, int color);

The function mask_pawn_attacks returns an attack mask that indicates where a pawn of the given color on the given square can attack.  This attack mask is determined from the position of the pawn and the color it is.  If it is white, then we need to apply right shift (move towards rank 8), if it is black, we need to apply left shift (move towards rank 1).  We need some guard clauses to prevent invalid attacks if our pawns are on the "a" or "h" files. 

U64 mask_knight_attacks(int square);

The function mask_knight_attacks returns an attack mask that indicates where a knight on the given square can attack.  The attack mask is generated relative to the bit that the knight is on in a bitboard.  Guard clauses are needed to prevent knights on files "a" and "b" as well as "g" and "h" from making invalid attacks. 

U64 mask_king_attacks(int square);

The function mask_king_attacks returns an attack mask indicating where a king on the given square can attack.  The attack mask is generated based on the bit that the king is on in the bitboard.  Guard clauses are needed to prevent kings on the "a" and "h" files from making certain illegal attacks. 

